Description: antinomie: keep the recorder out of the kernel's BTF
 A guest reads the kernel's BTF (/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux and one file per
 loaded module); Debian's kernel has it, and systemd's bpf-restrict-fs needs
 it. pahole builds it from the kernel's debug information, which would name
 the recorder's functions, types and the fields it adds to stock structures.
 .
 The recorder's objects are built without debug information, so pahole
 describes nothing of kernel/antinomie. What its headers bring into stock
 objects (task_struct's ant_* fields, io_kiocb's, the structures behind them,
 inline helpers) is removed by scripts/antinomie_btf.py right after pahole:
 every type, member, enumerator, function and variable with the recorder's
 name, what refers only to them, and their strings. A structure keeps its size
 and the offsets of its other members; the removed member is a hole.
 .
 Modules are encoded against the unfiltered vmlinux BTF, so they share the
 types the filter changed rather than carry copies of them, and are filtered
 onto the filtered one with the renumbering the vmlinux pass recorded.
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-28

---
diff --git a/kernel/antinomie/Makefile b/kernel/antinomie/Makefile
index 3ee011d..9f3944a 100644
--- a/kernel/antinomie/Makefile
+++ b/kernel/antinomie/Makefile
@@ -1,3 +1,9 @@
+# No debug information for the recorder's objects: pahole builds the kernel's
+# BTF (/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux, readable in the guest) from it, so none of the
+# recorder's functions, variables or types are described there. What its
+# headers bring into stock objects is filtered out (scripts/antinomie_btf.py).
+ccflags-remove-y += $(DEBUG_CFLAGS)
+
 # section_begin.o first and section_end.o last: they bracket this directory's
 # symbols in vmlinux (sections.h).
 obj-y += section_begin.o
